course: dictionary
lesson: 2968
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word drug can mean a medicine, a harmful substance, or a verb meaning to make someone sleep. Look at the words around it to decide.
explain: «Detection dogs are trained to sniff out things such as drugs, explosives, fruit carried in bags, money and even some illnesses.» The list tells you drugs are things a dog can find by smell. Drugs here means substances that are hidden or dangerous, not a cure. Try your meaning in the sentence: does "dogs are trained to sniff out substances" make sense? Yes, so it fits.
explain: «Substance misuse» is the official phrase for harmful use of drugs or alcohol. The word harmful is the clue: drugs can hurt you when they are taken in the wrong way. Always ask what the word is doing in the sentence, then check your meaning fits.
explain: Words can change their job too. Drug can be a noun (a drug, drugs) or a verb (to drug, drugged). When drug is a verb, it means giving someone a drug to make them sleep or unable to act. If you are not sure, look the word up in a dictionary.
fact: «Drug» can mean a medicine used to treat or prevent an illness.
fact: «Drug» can also mean a substance that changes how the body or mind works, and that people may take in a harmful or illegal way.
fact: Only take medicine from a trusted adult, and never take unknown pills or drugs.
fact: Drugs are tested before doctors use them, so that they are safe and work properly.
fact: «In recovery» means recovering from an addiction, such as to alcohol or drugs, and staying off it.
exercise: «Detection dogs are trained to sniff out things such as drugs, explosives, fruit carried in bags, money and even some illnesses.» In this sentence, drugs are | Animals that live in the sea | *Things a dog can be trained to find by its sense of smell | Sweets that dogs like to eat | Bags that hold fruit
exercise: «Substance misuse» is the British official phrase for harmful use of drugs or alcohol. What does harmful mean here? | Free to use | Very tasty | *Likely to hurt or damage
exercise: To drug someone means to | Take them to the shops | *Give them a drug to make them sleep or unable to act | Teach them to swim | Give them a present
test: «In recovery» means recovering from an addiction, such as to alcohol or drugs, and staying off it. In this phrase, recovery means | *Getting better from an addiction and staying off it | Running very fast | Going on holiday | Buying a new house
test: A doctor says a pill is safe for you. Which rule should you follow? | Try unknown pills to see how they feel | *Only take medicine from a trusted adult | Share your medicine with everyone at school | Take any pill a friend offers you
exam: «Then Britain kept selling opium, a harmful drug, into China.» In this sentence, drug means | A dog trained to sniff things out | A type of tea grown in China | A silver coin used by traders | *A harmful substance that changes how the body or mind works
